sea of thieves is often at its best  when it turns into total chaos imagine  sinking a ship  manned by skeletons or diving into a  sunken wreck loading your brig up with  all the loot you find  suddenly a huge galleon manned by four  players appears from behind an island  intent on stealing the booty you just  got done stealing  the galleon turns its broadside cannons  in your direction  for a sneak attack returning fire  repairing your ship and desperately  turning your sails to make an escape or  firing a crewmate over to the enemy to  lay waste to them and steal their stuff  are the moments that make rare's pirate  adventure live game  so memorable  but even though a lot of sea of thieves  fun comes from  unpredictable interactions with other  players developer rare has also created  some involved  intelligent story content for the game  as well called tall  tales with its new expansion a pirate's  life rare adds five tall tales to sea of  thieves that tie in with disney's  pirates of the caribbean film franchise  a pirate's life adds a lot to sea of  thieves  with new elements for its open world  sandbox and several  hours of story content it's deep enough  for experienced pirates to enjoy  but approachable enough for newbies  excited to sail the seas  with captain jack sparrow though the  expansion is occasionally held back by  bugs and relies on some of sea of  thieves weaker elements  like unreliable platforming the  expansion is still a great excuse to try  your hand  or hook at the pirate's life the core of  the expansion are the five new  tall tales which have you meeting up  with pirates of the caribbean mainstay  jack sparrow and his crew  scandals villains and knives streak up  me hearties  [Music]  through some kind of adventuring the  movie characters have found their way  into the weird and mystical world of the  video game but they've been chased there  by davey jones  the magical pirates of the caribbean  villain jones wants to take control of  the sea of thieves  so you need to save jack from the game's  realm of the dead figure out jones plans  and ultimately send the squid-faced  villain back to where he belongs  playing through these stories takes you  to two new locations  the sea of the damned full of puzzles  and secrets and the sunken kingdom a  colorful  underwater realm the tall tales  themselves are  pretty linear story levels but like sea  of thieves other tall tales  they do a great job of challenging you  to explore the environment  solve puzzles and navigate imaginative  areas  in the first for instance you'll need to  rescue the head of an  undead skeletal pirate captain and you  can uncover secrets throughout the area  by placing his head on skeleton bodies  so he can take control of them  of course this is a pirates of the  caribbean story so you'll spend a lot of  the duration  encountering various movie characters  jack sparrow is around the most  and thanks to some funny lines and  exciting gameplay moments  he's a pretty good addition captain jack  sparrow  though since you came here to rescue me  you probably knew that already  sparrow's presence helps meld the disney  aspects of the expansion  with sea of thieves foundation which  helps to make sure the content doesn't  feel like a digression  into another franchise he's the same  funny quirky character from the movies  but he's also pretty useful at key  moments even joining your ship's crew  for a while  he's around just enough to make this  feel like a pirates of the caribbean  story  while also working as a fitting sea of  thieves story  a pirate's life is also a useful entry  point for new players  the tall tales mostly take place off the  beaten path  or in their own instances so you're  unlikely to run into other players  who might try to steal your stuff sink  your boat or otherwise hassle you  the stakes are also pretty low anytime  you die you just respawn in the same  place in the tall tale story  without losing any progress that makes  this content pretty great for just  getting started in sea of thieves  or for lone players who want to venture  into the game without risks raised by  other pirates  the drawback is that a pirate's life  doesn't really feel  like the rest of sea of thieves it's  more of its own  separate thing in the rest of rare's  game sailing around requires constant  vigilance as you scope for ships and  other potential threats  the openness of the game and the  randomness created by other players  are what makes sea of thieves so fun and  unique  a pirate's life doesn't really include  that aspect  the stories are more straightforward and  traditional video game fare  you'll move through distinct levels on a  specific path  and while there are side puzzles and  secrets along the way  there's still only one way forward so a  pirate's life gives  a sense of sea of thieves but it's  incomplete  without other players that can be good  and bad  since a pirate's life eases you into the  water rather than shoving you off the  plank  but if you're new it can also give a  skewed perception of what the game  is the tall tales for a pirate's life  are  also a bit on the buggy side at launch  although rare has already released a  patch  to clean up some of the issues  [Music]  still when i played there were a few  game affecting oddities  like checkpoints that didn't work  falling from high places and finding  yourself stuck behind locked doors that  you'd already opened  or fights where elements didn't reset  correctly after dying  still i played through all five tall  tales alone  and didn't encounter any game breaking  issues the other upshot of a pirate's  life is the way it reinvigorates  the rest of the game the expansion  introduces a bunch of new enemies to the  game world that shake up your usual  sea of thieves adventures undersea  sirens make jumping off your boat all  the more treacherous  while on land you'll have to deal with  phantom pirates who can briefly make  themselves invisible  there are also three giant human  crustacean hybrid creatures called ocean  crawlers  an armored crab a poisonous clam and an  electric eel  all three require distinct strategies to  defeat  and they can synergize their abilities  to add additional challenge  to help you deal with these threats you  can find a new item called the trident  of dark tides  which charges up to fire a bubble of  energy  that can be devastating to ai enemies  and other players alike  for most of the life of sea of thieves  players have been facing a variety of  skeleton pirates all of which fight in  more or less the same way so throwing in  some new creatures  livens things up quite a bit and if you  can find it in the world  the trident of dark tides also creates  new strategies in combat  it all adds a little more depth to help  make the game continue to feel like it's  evolving  all the time a pirate's life is a big  fun addition to sea of thieves  that offers a lot of content both for  existing players  and fans of disney's pirates it doesn't  quite represent the best of what sea of  thieves has to offer  but this is still a great excuse to  hoist the pirate flag for the first time  and offers a new horizon to explore for  long time  [Music]  scalawags  [Music]  you
 

